\title{Tangled Hierarchies, Predicative Levels, and the Diagonal Boundary}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 22, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Hierarchical systems often exhibit apparent feedback: an object may be re-expressed at a higher level and later recovered, while polymorphic operations recur across many levels. We separate such reversible changes of presentation from genuine cycles in strict dependency and from unrestricted semantic self-representation. A tangle in a relation is a pair $x,y$ satisfying both $x\mathrel r y$ and $y\mathrel r x$. Well-founded relations exclude tangles. More constructively, a natural-valued rank that strictly decreases along dependency edges establishes well-foundedness, forces strict rank descent along every nonempty finite path, and excludes finite cycles of every length. Applied to universe indices ordered by $<$, this shows that the hierarchy $\mathcal U_0,\mathcal U_1,\ldots$ is well-founded, untangled, unbounded, and without a maximal level.

Universe raising is then modeled as a higher-level copy $L(A)$ equivalent to $A$. Raising followed by lowering preserves every value, and iterated raising is coherent with iterated projection. Such round trips are presentation loops, not reverse edges in universe order. Finally, a Cantor--Lawvere diagonal argument shows that no set of codes can represent every predicate on itself. More generally, point-surjective self-representation $R:C\to(C\to O)$ forces every endomorphism of $O$ to possess a fixed point. These results identify three distinct logical strengths: cyclic dependency, reversible transport, and universal semantic coding.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Hierarchies organize dependency. In a stratified language, objects occupy levels; in recursive computation, calls descend through a measure; in a build graph, prerequisites precede their consumers. Sophisticated systems also contain feedback-like behavior. A program manipulates programs, a language describes its own expressions, or an object is transported to a larger ambient level and returned. At least three structures can therefore look like loops:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item a directed cycle in strict dependency;
\item mutually inverse or retracting maps between presentations of the same data;
\item internal coding rich enough to represent all observations about its own codes.
\end{enumerate}
Their logical behavior differs sharply. A dependency cycle contradicts an asymmetric hierarchy law. A reversible representation change can be benign. Universal semantic representation supports diagonal substitution and may force impossible fixed points.

A strict partial order cannot contain $x<y$ and $y<x$, since strict orders are asymmetric. We therefore define tangling for an arbitrary binary relation and then establish its incompatibility with well-foundedness, rank descent, or asymmetry. This avoids concealing the contradiction inside the terminology.

The results below distinguish an unbounded but acyclic universe ladder from reversible transport across that ladder, and both from unrestricted semantic self-coding. The diagonal conclusion is a structural obstruction relevant to self-typing systems; it is not a complete derivation of paradox for every dependent calculus. Such a derivation would require a specified calculus and its precise closure rules.

\section{Relations and genuine tangles}

Let $X$ be a collection and $r\subseteq X\times X$ a relation. Read $x\mathrel r y$ as ``$x$ depends strictly on $y$'' or ``$x$ lies below $y$.''

\begin{definition}[Tangle]
The relation $r$ is \emph{tangled} if there exist $x,y\in X$ for which
\[
x\mathrel r y\quad\text{and}\quad y\mathrel r x.
\]
No order laws are assumed.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Two-cycle characterization]
A relation $r$ is tangled if and only if there are $x_0,x_1,x_2\in X$ such that
\[
x_2=x_0,\qquad x_0\mathrel r x_1,\qquad x_1\mathrel r x_2.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
From a tangle choose $x_0=x$, $x_1=y$, and $x_2=x$. Conversely, substitute $x_2=x_0$ in the closed walk.
\end{proof}

A relation is well-founded when recursive descent along it cannot continue indefinitely. In standard set-theoretic settings, this excludes an infinite sequence $x_0,x_1,\ldots$ with $x_{n+1}\mathrel r x_n$ for every $n$.

\begin{theorem}[Well-founded relations are untangled]
If $r$ is well-founded, there are no $x,y\in X$ satisfying both $x\mathrel r y$ and $y\mathrel r x$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A well-founded strict relation is asymmetric. Hence $x\mathrel r y$ excludes $y\mathrel r x$. Equivalently, the two edges could be alternated forever to produce an infinite descent.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A tangle forces hierarchy failure]
If $r$ is tangled, then it cannot satisfy
\[
\forall x,y\in X,\quad x\mathrel r y\Longrightarrow\neg(y\mathrel r x).
\]
Consequently, the asymmetry law and a tangle together imply contradiction.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose tangle witnesses $x,y$. Asymmetry applied to $x\mathrel r y$ gives $\neg(y\mathrel r x)$, contrary to the reverse edge.
\end{proof}

Thus a consistent account must discard the genuine cycle or weaken the strict hierarchy law.

\section{Ranks and exclusion of every finite cycle}

\begin{definition}[Natural-ranked hierarchy]
A natural-ranked hierarchy consists of $X$, a relation $r$, and a rank $\rho:X\to\N$ such that
\[
x\mathrel r y\Longrightarrow\rho(x)<\rho(y).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Rank descent implies well-foundedness]
The dependency relation of every natural-ranked hierarchy is well-founded.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
An infinite descent $x_{n+1}\mathrel r x_n$ would induce
\[
\rho(x_0)>\rho(x_1)>\rho(x_2)>\cdots,
\]
an infinite strictly decreasing sequence of natural numbers. No such sequence exists.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Ranked hierarchies are untangled]
A natural-ranked hierarchy contains no tangle.
\end{corollary}

Let $x_0,x_1,\ldots,x_n$ be a finite dependency path, meaning $x_{i+1}\mathrel r x_i$ whenever $0\leq i<n$.

\begin{theorem}[Finite-path rank descent]
If $n>0$, then every path in a natural-ranked hierarchy satisfies
\[
\rho(x_n)<\rho(x_0).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $n$. One edge gives the inequality directly. For the induction step, combine rank descent along the prefix with the strict decrease along the last edge, using transitivity of $<$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No positive-length finite cycle]
No dependency path of length $n>0$ in a natural-ranked hierarchy can satisfy $x_n=x_0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Finite-path descent gives $\rho(x_n)<\rho(x_0)$. Substituting $x_n=x_0$ yields $\rho(x_0)<\rho(x_0)$, contradicting irreflexivity.
\end{proof}

Ranks therefore provide local certificates of a global fact. Checking strict decrease on every edge excludes every finite cycle. For a finite graph with edge set $E$, this check requires $O(|E|)$ comparisons. Natural rankability is stronger than mere acyclicity for arbitrary infinite relations: some well-founded relations require ordinal-valued ranks.

\section{Predicative universe levels}

Model levels by $\N$ and define level $i$ to be below level $j$ precisely when $i<j$. This describes the schematic ladder
\[
\mathcal U_0:\mathcal U_1:\mathcal U_2:\cdots.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Well-founded universe indices]
The relation $<$ on natural-number universe indices is well-founded in the dependency direction.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Natural numbers have no infinite strictly decreasing sequence; equivalently, induction establishes accessibility of every index.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Universe levels are untangled]
There are no indices $i,j$ such that $i<j$ and $j<i$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded universe levels]
Every universe index has a strictly higher index:
\[
\forall i\in\N\ \exists j\in\N,\quad i<j.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $j=i+1$.
\end{proof}

Thus the hierarchy is infinite and has no maximal index, yet remains acyclic and well-founded in the downward direction. Unbounded ascent is not circularity.

\subsection{Reversible changes of presentation}

For a collection $A$ at one level, let $L(A)$ be a lifted copy at a higher level. An element $a\in A$ has a wrapper $\operatorname{up}(a)\in L(A)$, and a lifted value has an underlying value obtained by $\operatorname{down}:L(A)\to A$. These maps exhibit an equivalence of represented data.

\begin{theorem}[Single-lift coherence]
For every $a\in A$,
\[
\operatorname{down}(\operatorname{up}(a))=a.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Lowering is the inverse of the canonical raising map on the lifted copy, so the inverse law applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Double-lift coherence]
For every $a\in A$, two lifts followed by two projections recover $a$:
\[
\operatorname{down}\!\left(\operatorname{down}\!\left(
\operatorname{up}(\operatorname{up}(a))\right)\right)=a.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply single-lift coherence to the outer wrapper and then to the inner wrapper.
\end{proof}

The equalities are closed paths among presentations, not cycles in the strict index order. Raising changes ambient presentation; lowering removes a wrapper. Neither operation asserts that a higher index lies below a lower one. A polymorphic operation may be instantiated at many levels without identifying those levels.

\section{The diagonal boundary}

Let $C$ be a set of codes. A predicate on $C$ is a map $C\to\Prop$. A representation scheme is
\[
R:C\to(C\to\Prop).
\]
It is unrestricted if it is surjective.

\begin{theorem}[No unrestricted predicate self-representation]
For every $C$, no map $R:C\to(C\to\Prop)$ is surjective.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $R$ is surjective and define
\[
D(c)\Longleftrightarrow\neg R(c)(c).
\]
Choose $d$ with $R(d)=D$. Evaluation at $d$ gives
\[
R(d)(d)\Longleftrightarrow D(d)\Longleftrightarrow\neg R(d)(d),
\]
a contradiction.
\end{proof}

The theorem does not prohibit encoding many predicates or all predicates expressible in a restricted language. It prohibits a code set from surjecting onto the full predicate space on itself.

The underlying fixed-point principle is more general. Let $O$ be an observation set and let $R:C\to(C\to O)$ represent every map from codes to observations.

\begin{theorem}[Self-representation fixed-point theorem]
If $R:C\to(C\to O)$ is surjective, then every transformation $t:O\to O$ has a fixed point: there exists $o\in O$ such that $t(o)=o$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Define $g:C\to O$ by $g(c)=t(R(c)(c))$. Choose $d$ such that $R(d)=g$, and put $o=R(d)(d)$. Then
\[
o=R(d)(d)=g(d)=t(R(d)(d))=t(o).
\]
\end{proof}

Taking $O=\Prop$ and $t$ to be negation recovers the preceding impossibility because negation has no fixed truth value. The dangerous ingredient is therefore point-surjective semantic evaluation, not self-reference or reversible lifting alone.

\section{Computational diagnostics}

\begin{algorithm}[Rank-certificate validation]
Given vertices $V$, edges $E$, and $\rho:V\to\N$, inspect every edge $(c,p)$. Reject if $\rho(c)\geq\rho(p)$; otherwise accept. Acceptance certifies the absence of every finite directed cycle. The running time is $O(|E|)$ and rank storage is $O(|V|)$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Two-cycle detection]
Store edges in a hash set. For each $(x,y)$, test whether $(y,x)$ is present. A match witnesses a tangle. Expected running time and storage are $O(|E|)$. This local test does not detect longer cycles.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Finite diagonal construction]
Represent $n$ coded predicates by an $n\times n$ Boolean table. Construct $D_i=\neg R_{ii}$. The vector $D$ differs from row $i$ at coordinate $i$, hence is absent from the list. Construction is $O(n)$; explicit comparison with all rows is $O(n^2)$.
\end{algorithm}

\section{Applications and discussion}

For terminating recursion, syntax depth, input size, or remaining fuel can serve as rank. Finite-path descent explains aggregate progress and excludes return to the same state. Build systems similarly use stages to certify that prerequisites cannot form a cycle.

Staged languages and metaprogramming often move between object language and metalanguage. Reversible conversions should not be mistaken for hierarchy collapse: safety depends on preserving an external grade and restricting semantic evaluation enough to avoid universal self-coding. Reflective systems evade diagonal contradiction through partial evaluation, stratified truth, restricted code spaces, intensional distinctions, or simple failure of surjectivity.

The results support three independent axes. On the \emph{dependency axis}, reverse strict edges contradict asymmetry and natural ranks exclude all finite cycles. On the \emph{presentation axis}, equivalent copies may be transported between ambient levels without changing level inequalities. On the \emph{semantic axis}, point-surjective self-representation activates diagonalization and forces universal fixed points.

A slogan such as $\mathcal U:\mathcal U$ may bundle self-classification, dependent-product closure, impredicative quantification, decoding, and extensionality. The diagonal theorem gives a contradiction whenever these principles provide unrestricted predicate representation. It is not a complete syntactic derivation for an unspecified calculus. Establishing a minimal Girard-style boundary requires defining such a calculus and deriving the representation interface from its rules.

\section{Future directions}

Natural next steps include characterizing when countable, finitely branching well-founded dependencies admit natural ranks and when ordinal ranks are necessary. A compositional language of lifts, projections, products, and dependent sums may admit a normalization theorem in which every closed transport loop acts identically on represented values while its level graph remains acyclic.

For self-coding calculi, one should determine when dependent-product closure and decoding entail point-surjective evaluation. A sharp minimality analysis should vary self-typing, product closure, and extensionality independently. Finally, the least ordinal rank decreasing along edges may quantify the cost of untangling and provide an invariant for comparing dependency networks.

\section{Conclusion}

Well-foundedness excludes two-cycles, while natural rank descent excludes every positive-length finite dependency cycle. Natural-number universe indices consequently form an infinite, unbounded, but untangled hierarchy. Raising and lowering preserve represented values because they change presentation rather than reverse level order. Universal semantic self-representation is stronger: diagonalization forces fixed points for every observation transformation and becomes impossible for predicates because negation has none.

Safe self-reference therefore preserves a decreasing rank, separates grades from representation maps, or restricts semantic coverage. The ladder may support journeys up and down; it cannot consistently make a rung strictly below itself or enumerate every truth about its own enumeration.

\end{document}

